# slug -> (summary, why artists painted it). Written for curious kids; keep claims safe and checkable.
T = {
"classical-greece": ("In 490 BC a small Athenian army beat the mighty Persian Empire at Marathon, 300 Spartans held the pass at Thermopylae, and the Greek fleet crushed Persia at Salamis. Free Athens then blossomed: Pericles built the Parthenon, citizens voted on laws, and thinkers like Socrates, Plato and Aristotle asked questions we still argue about, until a long war with Sparta ended the golden age.",
  "Later painters used Greece as a picture of what free people can do. David showed Leonidas and Socrates as models of courage, and Raphael painted all the great philosophers together in one room to say the Renaissance was ancient Athens reborn."),
"_old-persian-wars": ("In 490 BC a small Athenian army beat the mighty Persian Empire at the Battle of Marathon. Ten years later 300 Spartans and their allies held the narrow pass at Thermopylae, and the Greek fleet destroyed Persia's ships at Salamis. Greece stayed free, and Athens went on to invent democracy and some of the world's greatest ideas.",
  "Artists have loved this story for centuries because it is about courage against huge odds. Painters like Jacques-Louis David used it to say that free citizens will fight harder than any king's army."),
"golden-age-athens": ("In the 400s BC Athens was the cleverest city on Earth. Citizens voted on laws in public, Pericles built the Parthenon, and thinkers like Socrates, Plato and Aristotle asked big questions that people are still arguing about. The golden age ended with a long war against Sparta, but the ideas lasted.",
  "Later painters used Athens as a picture of what human thinking could be. When Raphael painted all the great philosophers in one room, he was saying that the Renaissance was ancient Athens reborn."),
"alexander": ("Alexander of Macedon became king at twenty and, in just twelve years, conquered Persia, Egypt and lands as far as India without losing a major battle. He spread Greek language and ideas across the known world, then died in Babylon at thirty-two. His generals split his empire, but Greek culture lived on in it for centuries.",
  "Alexander is the model of the young hero-conqueror. Painters showed him taming his horse Bucephalus, cutting the Gordian knot and being kind to his enemy's captured family, scenes that mix glory with mercy."),
"early-rome": ("Rome began, so the legend goes, when twin brothers Romulus and Remus, raised by a wolf, founded a city on seven hills in 753 BC. After the last king was thrown out, Rome became a Republic run by elected leaders. Stories of heroic early Romans taught later generations what it meant to be a good citizen.",
  "In the 1700s, painters like David and Poussin used these Roman tales to show duty, sacrifice and loyalty to your country. The pictures were also political: they said the people, not a king, should rule."),
"punic-wars": ("Rome and Carthage, a great trading city in North Africa, fought three wars between 264 and 146 BC. The Carthaginian general Hannibal led an army with war elephants over the Alps and crushed the Romans at Cannae, but Rome never gave up. In the end Scipio beat Hannibal and Rome destroyed Carthage, becoming master of the Mediterranean.",
  "Hannibal's elephants crossing the Alps is one of history's most dramatic scenes. Artists like Turner used it to show how small people look against nature and fate."),
"caesar": ("Julius Caesar won Gaul, crossed the Rubicon river in 49 BC with his army, beat his rival Pompey and made himself dictator of Rome. Many senators feared he wanted to be king, and on the Ides of March in 44 BC a group of them stabbed him to death. The civil wars that followed ended the Roman Republic for good.",
  "Few events have been painted so often. Artists were fascinated by Caesar's fall: the moment when power, friendship and fear all collided in a single room."),
"augustus-cleopatra": ("After Caesar died, his adopted son Octavian fought Mark Antony, who had joined forces with Cleopatra, the last queen of Egypt. Octavian won the Battle of Actium in 31 BC, Egypt became part of Rome, and he became Augustus, the first Roman emperor. His reign started two hundred years of peace known as the Pax Romana.",
  "Cleopatra's story mixes love, power and tragedy, which made it a favorite subject for painters from the Baroque era onward. Many pictures show her as glamorous; real Cleopatra was a brilliant ruler who spoke many languages."),
"imperial-rome": ("At its height the Roman Empire stretched from Britain to Egypt, with roads, aqueducts, theaters and the giant Colosseum, where gladiators fought in front of tens of thousands. In 79 AD, Mount Vesuvius buried the towns of Pompeii and Herculaneum, freezing everyday Roman life in ash for almost 1,700 years.",
  "The 1800s were mad about Rome. Painters like Lawrence Alma-Tadema made super-detailed pictures of Roman daily life, using what archaeologists were digging up at Pompeii."),
"fall-of-rome": ("In the 300s and 400s AD, the Western Roman Empire was weakened by civil wars, money troubles and pressure from peoples like the Goths, Vandals and Huns. Rome itself was sacked in 410 and 455, and in 476 the last emperor in the West was removed. The Eastern Empire, called Byzantium, carried on for another thousand years.",
  "Artists have used Rome's fall as a warning ever since. In The Course of Empire, Thomas Cole showed a great city rising and falling, and told Americans that no nation is safe forever."),
"charlemagne": ("Charlemagne, king of the Franks, ruled much of Western Europe from 768 to 814. On Christmas Day in 800, the Pope crowned him emperor in Rome, the first in the West since Rome's fall. He supported scholars and schools, and his court helped save many ancient books.",
  "Painters showed Charlemagne as the founder of Christian Europe: a wise king surrounded by scholars and knights. In the 1800s he became a national hero for both the French and the Germans."),
"vikings": ("From about 793 to 1066, Norse sailors from Norway, Denmark and Sweden set out in their longships to raid, trade and settle. They reached England, France, Russia and Iceland, and Leif Erikson's crew landed in North America around the year 1000. Their sagas, gods and ships still capture the imagination.",
  "Norwegian and Scandinavian painters of the 1800s used the Viking age to say who they were as new nations. Their pictures of Harald Fairhair, Leif Erikson and Odin's hunt made the sagas look as real as yesterday."),
"norman-conquest": ("In 1066 the Norman duke William crossed the English Channel and defeated King Harold Godwinson at the Battle of Hastings. William became king of England and the Normans built castles, changed the law and brought French words to the English language. The famous Bayeux Tapestry tells the story in stitches.",
  "The year 1066 is the most famous date in English history. Victorian painters made scenes of Harold's death and William's landing to show where England's story began."),
"crusades": ("Beginning in 1095, European armies marched east to capture Jerusalem and the Holy Land. Over about 200 years there were many crusades, with famous leaders like Richard the Lionheart and Saladin. They changed trade, travel and ideas between Europe and the Middle East, but caused great suffering too.",
  "Romantic painters of the 1800s loved the Crusades as tales of knights and faraway adventures, often prettier than the real history. Looking closely, you can ask: whose side is the painter showing?"),
"hundred-years-war": ("From 1337 to 1453 England and France fought on and off in the Hundred Years' War. English archers won at Crécy and Agincourt, but a teenage peasant girl named Joan of Arc rallied the French and helped crown King Charles VII. By the end, England had lost almost all of its French lands.",
  "Joan of Arc became a saint and a national hero, so painters of the 1800s showed her as everything from a quiet farm girl hearing voices to a shining warrior on horseback."),
"black-death": ("In 1347 a terrible plague reached Europe on trading ships and killed perhaps a third of the people in about four years. Villages emptied, and the world changed: workers were suddenly scarce, so they could ask for higher pay. Art filled up with skeletons, dances of death and prayers for mercy.",
  "People then had no idea what caused the disease, so artists painted it as a mysterious visitor: Death himself walking among the living. These pictures are reminders of how fragile life seemed."),
"constantinople": ("For a thousand years after Rome fell in the West, the Byzantine Empire ruled from Constantinople (now Istanbul), a golden city with the great church of Hagia Sophia. In 1453 the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured it after a long siege. The fall of Constantinople is often used to mark the end of the Middle Ages.",
  "To artists, Constantinople stood for a dazzling lost world. Painters showed emperors, empresses and the mighty walls that held out for so long."),
"renaissance": ("In the 1400s and 1500s Italian cities like Florence became bursting with new ideas. Rich families like the Medici paid for artists and scholars who studied ancient Greece and Rome, discovered how to paint perspective, and made science, books and art flourish. The printing press helped the new ideas spread across Europe.",
  "The Renaissance was one of the most art-filled times in history. These pictures show the artists, patrons and thinkers who changed the world, from artists' own times and from later painters who looked back in awe."),
"discovery": ("In 1492 Christopher Columbus sailed west from Spain and reached the Caribbean. Over the next hundred years, Portuguese, Spanish, English and French explorers like da Gama, Magellan and Cortés crossed oceans and met peoples who had never been in contact with Europe. The Age of Discovery connected the world, but it also brought conquest, disease and hardship for Indigenous peoples.",
  "Artists of the 1800s made these voyages look like grand adventures. Today we can also ask who is missing from the picture and what the people who already lived there would have shown."),
"reformation": ("In 1517 a German monk named Martin Luther challenged the Church by posting his 95 Theses. With the help of the printing press, his ideas spread fast and Europe split into Catholic and Protestant lands. The Reformation changed religion, politics, education and even who could read the Bible.",
  "Protestant painters like Lucas Cranach made Luther's portrait famous in his own lifetime, and later artists painted dramatic scenes such as Luther standing alone before the Emperor at Worms."),
"armada": ("Elizabeth I ruled England from 1558 to 1603, a time of explorers, theater and Shakespeare. In 1588 King Philip II of Spain sent a huge fleet, the Armada, to invade England, but smaller English ships and fierce storms scattered it. The victory made England a sea power.",
  "Elizabeth's portraits were carefully designed to make her look powerful and almost magical. The famous Armada Portrait uses symbols and clothes to say: England rules the waves."),
"thirty-years-war": ("The Dutch rebelled against Spanish rule from 1568 and the Thirty Years' War (1618–1648) spread across Germany and beyond, one of Europe's deadliest conflicts. The Peace of Westphalia in 1648 ended both wars, made the Dutch Republic independent and started the modern idea of countries with borders.",
  "Some artists painted heroic generals; others, like Callot, showed how terrible war really is. Spain's Velázquez painted a famous scene of a defeated Dutch commander handing over the keys, with surprising kindness."),
"colonial-america": ("In 1620 the Mayflower brought about 100 English Pilgrims to Plymouth in what is now Massachusetts. With help from the Wampanoag people, they survived their first winters and shared the harvest celebration we call the first Thanksgiving. Soon more colonies grew along the Atlantic coast.",
  "Painters of the 1800s turned the Pilgrims into America's origin story, often showing them as calm, devout and brave. Their pictures became part of how Americans remember Thanksgiving."),
"english-civil-war": ("King Charles I and Parliament went to war in 1642 over who should rule England. Oliver Cromwell's army won, and in 1649 the king was put on trial and executed. England became a republic for a few years, then brought back a king in 1660; in 1688 the Glorious Revolution settled that Parliament has the final say.",
  "Painters loved the contrast between royal elegance and the grim fight that ended it. The scenes of Charles I's last days and Cromwell's soldiers are some of the most emotional pictures in British art."),
"peter-the-great": ("Peter the Great, tsar of Russia from 1682 to 1725, was over six and a half feet tall and wanted his country to catch up with Europe. He traveled abroad to learn shipbuilding, defeated Sweden at Poltava in 1709 and built a brand-new capital, Saint Petersburg, on swampy land beside the Baltic Sea.",
  "Russian painters of the 1800s, like Repin and Serov, made Peter the star of Russian history paintings. They showed both his energy and his harshness."),
"enlightenment": ("In the 1600s and 1700s curious people began to explore the world with experiments and reason. Galileo looked at the sky with a telescope, Newton explained gravity, and Enlightenment thinkers like Voltaire and Diderot argued for freedom of thought. Their ideas inspired revolutions and modern science.",
  "Painters in this age began to show scientists as heroes. Joseph Wright of Derby lit his experiments like religious scenes, as if the glow of discovery was holy."),
"new-france": ("France and Britain fought for control of North America in the Seven Years' War (1756–1763). In 1759 British soldiers under General James Wolfe climbed cliffs near Quebec City and won a short battle on the Plains of Abraham, though both Wolfe and the French commander Montcalm were killed. France gave up Canada, and the map of North America changed.",
  "Benjamin West's 'Death of General Wolfe' made the battle famous and started a trend of showing modern heroes in realistic clothes. Canadians and Americans still see this moment in his picture."),
"american-revolution": ("In 1775 American colonists began fighting for freedom from Britain. On July 4, 1776 they declared independence, and after eight years of war, led by George Washington and helped by France, they won. The United States was born as a republic where leaders are chosen by the people.",
  "American painters, especially John Trumbull, were eager to show the founding to the world. Many of these scenes became so famous they are on money and in school books."),
"french-revolution": ("In 1789 the people of France rose up against the king and the old system of privilege. They stormed the Bastille prison, wrote the Declaration of the Rights of Man, and executed King Louis XVI in 1793. A time of terror followed, and the revolution ended when Napoleon took power in 1799.",
  "Jacques-Louis David was both a painter and a revolutionary, and his pictures turned politics into art. Later artists like Delacroix made liberty itself look like a heroine."),
"napoleon": ("Napoleon Bonaparte rose from a Corsican officer to emperor of France in 1804. His armies won huge victories at Austerlitz and Jena but failed in Russia in 1812, and he was finally beaten at Waterloo in 1815 and exiled to Saint Helena. His laws and ideas still shape Europe.",
  "Napoleon hired the greatest painters of his age to make him look like a god-hero, while others, like Goya, painted what his wars really did to people."),
"industrial-revolution": ("From about 1760, steam engines, iron, coal and factories changed how people worked, traveled and lived. Railways crossed continents, cities exploded in size and goods that were once made by hand came pouring out of machines. It made some people rich and left many in smoky, crowded towns.",
  "Artists were amazed and troubled by the new world. Turner painted a steam train racing through rain, while others showed the glow of ironworks, the hard work of laborers and the beauty of bridges."),
"independence-revolutions": ("Between 1820 and 1848, waves of revolt swept across the world. Greeks fought for independence from the Ottoman Empire, South American countries from Spain, and in 1830 and 1848 the people of France and much of Europe rose up for more freedom. Many of these revolutions failed at first, but their ideas about nations and rights did not go away.",
  "Romantic artists took sides. Delacroix painted Greeks suffering at Chios and Liberty marching over barricades, and artists in Latin America celebrated their own heroes like Simón Bolívar."),
"unifications": ("Italy and Germany were once collections of small states. In the 1800s patriots like Garibaldi, Cavour and Bismarck joined them into single nations: Italy by 1861 (with Rome in 1870), and Germany in 1871, when its emperor was crowned in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles.",
  "Painters of both countries made heroic pictures to build national pride. Garibaldi's red-shirted volunteers and the German imperial proclamation became symbols of a new national identity."),
"crimean-war": ("From 1853 to 1856, Britain, France and the Ottoman Empire fought Russia in the Crimea, on the Black Sea. It is remembered for the doomed Charge of the Light Brigade, for Florence Nightingale's nursing and as one of the first wars photographed. Many more soldiers died of disease than of wounds.",
  "Crimea was the first war the public could follow in newspapers, photographs and paintings. Pictures like 'The Thin Red Line' and 'Balaclava' made ordinary soldiers into heroes."),
"abolition": ("For hundreds of years, millions of Africans were taken across the Atlantic and forced into slavery. Brave people, from enslaved fighters like Harriet Tubman and Frederick Douglass to campaigners like William Wilberforce, worked to end it. Britain ended the slave trade in 1807 and slavery in its empire in 1833, and the United States ended slavery in 1865.",
  "Some artists used their work to make people feel the cruelty of slavery, like Turner's wild 'Slave Ship'. Others later honored the escape and the long fight for freedom."),
"us-civil-war": ("From 1861 to 1865 the United States fought a terrible civil war between the Union and the slaveholding Confederacy. Abraham Lincoln's Union won, ended slavery and kept the country together, but about 620,000 soldiers died. Photography let people see the war's reality for the first time.",
  "Winslow Homer began as a war illustrator and made some of the most honest pictures of Civil War soldiers. Other artists painted Lincoln, the great battles and the last days of the war."),
"westward-expansion": ("In the 1800s settlers, miners, railroads and cowboys moved across North America to the Pacific. The Louisiana Purchase, the Oregon Trail, the Gold Rush and the Transcontinental Railroad opened the West, while Indigenous nations lost their lands, often by force. In Canada, a railroad stitched the country together after Confederation in 1867.",
  "Artists like Albert Bierstadt and Frederic Remington created the American West as a place of mountains, wagon trains, cowboys and epic skies, a picture that still shapes how we imagine it."),
"franco-prussian-war": ("In 1870 France declared war on Prussia and lost quickly. Emperor Napoleon III was captured at Sedan, Paris was besieged and starved for four months, and Germany became an empire. Then Parisians rose in a short-lived revolt called the Paris Commune, which was crushed in a week of bloody fighting.",
  "French painters like Meissonier and Detaille turned the defeat into pictures of courage, loss and hope for revenge. Their huge, detailed canvases are history lessons you can walk into."),
"suffrage": ("For centuries women were not allowed to vote. In 1848 a meeting at Seneca Falls, New York, called for women's rights, and campaigns grew in Britain, the United States and Scandinavia. New Zealand allowed women to vote in 1893, Norway in 1913, Canada from 1917 to 1919 and the United States in 1920.",
  "Posters, banners and paintings were important weapons for the suffrage movement. Colorful art turned a hard political fight into pictures that anyone could understand."),
"wwi": ("World War I (1914–1918) pulled in most of the world's great powers and was fought in muddy trenches from Belgium to Russia and across the seas. New weapons like machine guns, poison gas and airplanes made it deadlier than any earlier war. Around 20 million people died, and it was called 'the war to end all wars'.",
  "Official war artists were sent to the front, and many returned with unforgettable pictures of soldiers, wrecked landscapes and the strange beauty of battlefields."),
"russian-revolution": ("In 1917 Russia's tsar was overthrown in the February Revolution, and in October the Bolsheviks under Lenin seized power. A civil war followed, and in 1922 the Soviet Union was formed. The revolution changed Russia, and for most of the 1900s it also shaped world politics.",
  "Artists were part of the revolution. Posters, banners and bold new styles were used to explain the new world, while painters like Kustodiev recorded the crowds in the streets."),
"great-depression": ("After the stock market crash of 1929, millions of people lost their jobs and savings. In the United States, the Dust Bowl blew away farmers' soil, and President Franklin Roosevelt's New Deal created work programs that built roads, parks and post offices, and hired artists to paint murals and take photographs.",
  "The government hired photographers like Dorothea Lange and Walker Evans to document hard times. Their pictures are now treasured as art, and they show people with great dignity."),
"wwii": ("World War II (1939–1945) was the largest war in history, fought across Europe, Africa, Asia and the Pacific. The Allies, including the United States, Britain, the Soviet Union and Canada, defeated Nazi Germany, Italy and Japan. The war included the Holocaust, in which six million Jews were murdered, and ended with the atomic bombs on Japan and the creation of the United Nations.",
  "The US Army and Navy sent artists to the front lines to record the war. These paintings and drawings, which belong to the American people, show soldiers, sailors and the home front."),
"moon-landing": ("In 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, and the Space Race began. In 1961 Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space, and on July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin of Apollo 11 became the first people to walk on the Moon while Michael Collins orbited above. Twelve Americans walked on the Moon in all.",
  "NASA photographs from the Moon are some of the most famous images of all time, and they count as art too: Earthrise and the Blue Marble changed how people see our planet."),
"vietnam-war": ("From 1965 to 1973 the United States fought in the Vietnam War, supporting South Vietnam against the Communist North. It was a long and costly conflict at home and overseas, with huge protests in the United States, and ended with US troops leaving in 1973 and the North taking the South in 1975.",
  "The US Army sent combat artists to Vietnam, just as it had in the world wars. Their paintings and drawings, made for the government and so in the public domain, show soldiers, helicopters and villages, and the quiet moments between the fighting."),
}
